n row 8. It looks like a blocked four, blocked on one side by G8 and open on the right (K8). If I play K8, it is not an immediate win because the diagonal is a more important threat. It won't win me the game because G8 blocks the other side.  But that's not my priority right now, since White's 4-in-a-row is what I must deal with.\n\n**The Forced Move - Blocking White's Threat:**\n\nWhite's diagonal is the priority. My only logical move to prevent a loss on the next turn is to block White's open end at D5.\n\n**Verifying the Solution and Final Decision:**\n\nI'm confirming that D5 is indeed the open end and that playing there will block White from winning. I can't let White make that connection! It's the only move that stops White's immediate win. There's no way to create a win or more favorable situation by playing anywhere else.\n\n**Constructing the Response:**\n\nSo, after careful evaluation, the only move to make here is D5. That will stop White from winning the game.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state shows that White has successfully built a diagonal line of four stones consisting of E6, F7, G8, and H9. This formation is known as a "Four". While the bottom-right end of this line is blocked by Black\'s stone at I10, the top-left end at D5 is currently empty. If Black does not occupy this square immediately, White will play there on the next turn to complete a "Five" (5-in-a-row) and win the game. Black\'s own offensive potential (a blocked three at H8-J8) is insufficient to win immediately or force a win before White strikes.\n\nReason: White has a lethal "Four" threat on the diagonal (E6, F7, G8, H9). Black must block the open end at D5 to survive.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "D", "row": 5}'}
